### Conditional means and variances {#sec-cond}

Alpha is half of a single effect's posterior. The other half is the effect size given its
feature, $q(b \mid \gamma = j) = N(\mu_j, \sigma_j^2)$. An arm that matches CAVI-Q2's alpha
and these conditionals reproduces the whole single-effect posterior. For each matched pair,
averaging over CAVI-Q2's $\alpha$:

- $|\Delta\mu| / \sigma = \sum_j \alpha_j |\mu'_j - \mu_j| / \sigma_j$, the shift in the
  conditional mean in units of CAVI-Q2's posterior SD.
- $\log \sigma'^2 / \sigma^2 = \sum_j \alpha_j \log(\sigma'^2_j / \sigma^2_j)$, signed:
  positive when the arm's conditional is wider. The figure uses its absolute value.
- $\text{KL}_{b \mid \gamma} = \sum_j \alpha_j \text{KL}(N(\mu_j, \sigma^2_j) \,\|\, N(\mu'_j,
  \sigma'^2_j))$. By the chain rule, $\text{KL}(\alpha \| \alpha') + \text{KL}_{b \mid \gamma}$
  is the KL between the two single-effect posteriors over $(\gamma, b)$, which the last figure
  shows.

As for TV, each distance has an every-pair figure, then one with rows for every matched pair
and for pairs whose smaller component log BF clears 0, 1, 2. Under the bar, both sides are close to the prior and the conditionals are
prior-dominated.

Separated pairs are left out of every figure and table on the conditionals. A feature
separates $y$ when all the rows with $x_j \neq 0$ (or all with $x_j = 0$) share one $y$; on
the binary designs this is a rare feature that is on in a handful of rows, all cases. Its
likelihood has no maximum, so the conditional mean and variance are whatever the prior allows
(the EB prior variance usually runs to its cap of 100, giving a mean near 10 and SD near 3.5). A pair is separated
when either side's top feature separates $y$, checked on the regenerated data. Arms then
differ mostly in how they treat the cap, which says nothing about the approximation.

