### Linear predictor {#sec-eta}

The comparisons above go through the matched components, one feature at a time. The linear
predictor $\eta_i = b_0 + \tilde x_i^\top b$ ($\tilde x$ the centered design, as fitted) needs
no matching and averages over $\alpha$: two fits that split an effect differently over
correlated features, but predict the same, agree on it. Under each fit's posterior, per
observation,

$$E[\eta_i] = m_0 + \sum_l \sum_j \alpha_{lj} \mu_{lj} \tilde x_{ij}, \qquad
\text{Var}[\eta_i] = v_0 + \sum_l \Big(\sum_j \alpha_{lj} (\mu_{lj}^2 + \sigma_{lj}^2) \tilde x_{ij}^2
- \big(\textstyle\sum_j \alpha_{lj} \mu_{lj} \tilde x_{ij}\big)^2\Big),$$

with $(m_0, v_0)$ the intercept's posterior mean and variance. Per fit, against CAVI-Q2 on the
same replicate:

- $|\Delta\eta| / \text{sd}$: the mean over observations of $|E'[\eta_i] - E[\eta_i]| /
  \text{sd}[\eta_i]$, the shift in CAVI-Q2 posterior SDs.
- $\log \text{Var}' / \text{Var}$: the mean over observations of
  $\log(\text{Var}'[\eta_i] / \text{Var}[\eta_i])$, signed: positive when the arm is wider.

Every fit counts, separated features included: they move only the handful of observations
where the feature is on. Signal cells only.

